    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d147">

                <pb facs="0156" n="144" sameAs="frus:frus1946v08/pg144"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 PW/2–2246</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Secretary of State to President
                        Truman</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-02-22">February 22, 1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>This<anchor n="89" xml:id="d147-f1"/> is similar to the document I recently
                    submitted to you. I am proposing to submit this draft to UK, USSR and China. I
                    have not included France. Will talk to you about it.<anchor n="90"
                        xml:id="d147-f2"/></p>
                <closer><signed>J[<hi rendition="#small-caps">ames</hi>] F. B[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">yrnes</hi>]</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d147-f1" n="89" xml:id="d147-f1-content">
                <p>See draft treaty, quoted in telegram 348, February 28, 3 p.m., to Moscow, <ref
                        target="#pg152">p. 152</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d147-f2" n="90" xml:id="d147-f2-content">
                <p>President Truman wrote his reply in the margin of this document as follows:
                    “Jim:—I [am] very anxious that our place in Pacific affairs should be a dominant
                    one in the peace as it has been in the war. H.S.T.”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
